1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,902
My name's Dan Pearman and I'd like to talk about the work of Pedal

2
00:00:03,902 --> 00:00:07,060
Power, a small charity based mainly in the UK.

3
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:11,468
I'll be giving our contact details at the end, if anyone would like to

4
00:00:11,468 --> 00:00:13,320
find out more about how to support us.

5
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:16,410
But first, how the charity began.

6
00:00:16,410 --> 00:00:21,768
I got the idea of exporting bicycles to developing countries while I was in

7
00:00:21,768 --> 00:00:22,190
Ecuador,

8
00:00:22,190 --> 00:00:26,860
I went there in 1993 just after graduating from university.

9
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:30,930
After three years of studying, I wanted adventure.

10
00:00:30,930 --> 00:00:32,076
I loved travelling,

11
00:00:32,076 --> 00:00:37,422
so I decided to join a voluntary organisation and was sent to Ecuador to

12
00:00:37,422 --> 00:00:38,950
carry out land surveys.

13
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:44,313
The project came to an end after five years and when I returned to

14
00:00:44,313 --> 00:00:47,760
the UK in 1998, I started planning Pedal Power.

15
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:52,060
Where I lived in Ecuador was a very rural area.

16
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:57,910
My neighbour had the only bicycle in the village, whereas everyone else walked everywhere.

17
00:00:57,910 --> 00:01:00,905
My neighbour's business was unusually successful,

18
00:01:00,905 --> 00:01:04,400
and for years I couldn't understand why.

19
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:08,608
Then I realised having a bike meant he could get where he wanted to

20
00:01:08,608 --> 00:01:09,810
go without much trouble.

21
00:01:09,810 --> 00:01:14,740
Other local carpenters could only accept jobs in a three-kilometre radius,

22
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:18,438
so no matter how skilled they were, they could never do as many jobs

23
00:01:18,438 --> 00:01:19,230
as my neighbour.

24
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:22,803
At Pedal Power, we collect second-hand bikes in the UK

25
00:01:22,803 --> 00:01:27,090
and send them to some of the poorest regions in the world.

26
00:01:27,090 --> 00:01:32,120
When we distribute bikes overseas we don't give them away for free.

27
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:33,089
We'd like to,

28
00:01:33,089 --> 00:01:36,320
but long term that doesn't really help the local economy.

29
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:38,910
The demand for bikes is enormous,

30
00:01:38,910 --> 00:01:41,500
which makes them very expensive locally.

31
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:45,170
So we sell them for 5% of the normal price.

32
00:01:45,170 --> 00:01:49,593
But in order to continue operating we need to have a constant supply of

33
00:01:49,593 --> 00:01:52,120
bikes which we send out every six months.

34
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:57,860
One example of a town that's received bicycles from Pedal Power is Rivas.

35
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:02,026
It was the first place I sent a full container of bicycles to.

36
00:02:02,026 --> 00:02:04,270
Most people there now own a bicycle.

37
00:02:04,270 --> 00:02:09,422
The local economy has developed so much, you wouldn't recognise it as the same

38
00:02:09,422 --> 00:02:09,790
place.

39
00:02:09,790 --> 00:02:14,130
In fact, there are more bikes than on the streets of Amsterdam, if you've

40
00:02:14,130 --> 00:02:15,060
ever been there.

41
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:17,540
But Pedal Power still needs your help.

42
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:22,500
You may have read about some of our recent problems in the British media.

43
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:26,330
In August 2000, we simply ran out of money.

44
00:02:26,330 --> 00:02:29,158
We had containers of bikes ready to send,

45
00:02:29,158 --> 00:02:31,632
but no money to pay the bills.

46
00:02:31,632 --> 00:02:33,400
It was a terrible situation.

47
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:37,784
We managed to ensure the bikes went out on time,

48
00:02:37,784 --> 00:02:41,730
but the other problems carried on for several months.

49
00:02:41,730 --> 00:02:48,000
Fortunately in October 2001 we won an Enterprise Award which helped us enormously.

50
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,560
We invested fifteen of the seventy-five-thousand-pound prize money to help secure our future.

51
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:57,229
Winning the award helped raise our profile,

52
00:02:57,229 --> 00:03:01,423
and the money enabled us to pay all our shipping costs,

53
00:03:01,423 --> 00:03:03,330
which represent our greatest expense.

54
00:03:03,330 --> 00:03:09,630
Pedal Power changes lives-when someone gets a bicycle from us, they see a 14%

55
00:03:09,630 --> 00:03:11,430
increase in their income.

56
00:03:11,430 --> 00:03:16,377
We're currently looking to invest in computers so that our office staff can do

57
00:03:16,377 --> 00:03:17,790
an even better job.

58
00:03:17,790 --> 00:03:23,468
Because of our work, people in a number of countries now have a better

59
00:03:23,468 --> 00:03:27,930
standard of living-so far we've provided 46,000 people with bikes.

60
00:03:27,930 --> 00:03:32,280
But we'd like to send more, at least 50,000 by the end of the

61
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:32,570
year.

62
00:03:32,570 --> 00:03:36,511
Now there are many ways in which you can support the work of Pedal

63
00:03:36,511 --> 00:03:40,170
Power, not just by taking a bike to a collection in your area.

64
00:03:40,170 --> 00:03:44,490
I should also like to say if you do have a bike to donate,

65
00:03:44,490 --> 00:03:46,650
it doesn't matter what condition it's in

66
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:50,560
-- if we can't repair it, we'll strip it down for spare parts.

67
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:53,432
Of course, to do that we always need tools,

68
00:03:53,432 --> 00:03:55,027
which are expensive to buy,

69
00:03:55,027 --> 00:03:57,580
so we welcome any that you can give.

70
00:03:57,580 --> 00:04:02,392
Also, you could help by contacting the voluntary staff at our offices, they'll be

71
00:04:02,392 --> 00:04:06,860
able to suggest activities you could organise to bring in funds for us.

72
00:04:06,860 --> 00:04:11,590
People do all kinds of things-including, of course, sponsored bike rides.

73
00:04:11,590 --> 00:04:16,926
Also, we're always interested to hear of other places that would benefit from receiving

74
00:04:16,926 --> 00:04:18,450
a consignment of bikes,

75
00:04:18,450 --> 00:04:23,000
and welcome suggestions from people who've been to developing regions on their travels.

76
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,133
We hope that by talking on radio programmes like this, we will be able

77
00:04:28,133 --> 00:04:29,600
to raise public awareness,

78
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:34,936
which will lead to government organisations also giving us regular financial support, something that

79
00:04:34,936 --> 00:04:36,080
we really need.

80
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:39,929
If you'd like some more information about where to donate an old bicycle

81
00:04:39,929 --> 00:04:42,890
or offer help in other ways please contact us on...
